# Line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
#   `core.autocrlf` is `true` on the machine that maintains this repo, and that
#   default converts text files between LF in the object database and CRLF in the
#   working tree. For hand-written source that is invisible. For *generated,
#   compared* artefacts it is not: a fresh `pg_dump -f` emits LF, the checkout it
#   replaces has been smudged to CRLF, and the two differ on every single line
#   while being byte-identical in substance.
#
#   This is not hypothetical. Regenerating `app_baseline/public_schema.sql` to
#   record migration 107 produced a 13,929-line diff in which 31 lines were the
#   actual change; the rest was line-ending damage accumulated because an earlier
#   capture had been written through a shell redirect rather than `pg_dump -f`
#   (that same redirect had also re-encoded UTF-8 and turned `§` into `Â§`). A
#   reviewer cannot audit a security-relevant schema capture inside a diff that
#   large, and the next capture would have hit it again.
#
# WHAT `-text` MEANS HERE
#   Store these files exactly as the generator produced them and never convert on
#   checkout, so blob and working tree are the same bytes. A later capture then
#   differs only where the database actually differs. This is the property the
#   baseline exists to provide: `schema:validate` and a code review of a capture
#   both assume the file is what the generator emitted.
#
# Everything else keeps git's normal behaviour; only the artefacts that are
# regenerated and diffed are pinned. There is deliberately no `merge=` or
# `diff=` driver here: naming a driver that is not configured in git makes git
# fail every conflicting change to these files rather than fall back to the
# normal three-way merge, which is a much worse trade than the one being fixed.

mock-tests/supabase/migrations/app_baseline/*.sql  -text
mock-tests/supabase/migrations/baseline/*.sql      -text

